    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Superconducting qubits are one of the leading platforms for quantum computing. While simple \"single-mode\" qubits such as the transmon or fluxonium are dominant in most large-scale applications, qubits with more degrees of freedom show great promise in achieving higher coherence times. However, it remains difficult to understand generic design principles for complicated circuits. Numerical optimization is a tempting approach, but faces many nuanced challenges. In this thesis, we present a general framework to perform such optimization robustly. In particular, we propose a well-supported method for truncating the infinite-dimensional circuit Hamiltonian which allows for faster optimization. We also offer heuristics on identifying gate charge sweet spots for generic circuits, supported by numerics. Finally, we show results from an optimization implementation using particle swarm, and demonstrate that it is able to effectively improve our desired metrics by orders of magnitude. Together, this provides start-to-end optimization which can easily be extended to new circuits and new design goals.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Bonkowsky, S. (2024). Towards Robust Optimization of Arbitrary Superconducting Qubits. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/ng701jh0332. https://doi.org/10.25740/ng701jh0332.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
